speechtext:
Mr. Speaker, the bill before us is to establish national standards for agricultural products, and to regulate international and interprovincial trade in agricultural products. As I stated a moment ago when we were on third reading of Bill No. 352, I intend to make some extended remarks in connection with the bill now before us.
The problem confronting the livestock industry in Canada deserves careful scrutiny by this government. While we have before us this bill to establish national standards for agricultural products and to provide grading and other provisions, I want to say that for a long time the national livestock industry in Canada has deserved better attention from this government than it has had. For many years this group in the house has advocated that if we are to get down to the basic problem of the producer, and indeed give an assurance that our consumers will receive these products in the best condition and at the most reasonable prices, then we are going to have to do something more than has been done in the past by this government.
I should like to bring to the attention of the house a portion of the report and recommendations of the royal commission on price

Agricultural Products
spreads of 1934. One of the important recommendations of that commission is contained on pages 172 and 173, as follows:
As a step toward the solution of some at least of the problems which we have outlined, we recommend the establishment of a livestock board, under the appropriate jurisdiction. The duties of the board should include, among others, the following:
(a)	The prompt dissemination of information to producers and the trade generally, in respect to production, marketing, stocks, and prices, both export and domestic;
(b)	Administrative jurisdiction in matters connected with all phases of livestock marketing and in connection with disputes between producers, processors, etc.;
(c)	Licensing and supervision of truckers, dealers and export packers; where necessary in co-operation with provincial authorities;
(d)	Adequate inspection of all marketing stages and action to correct abuses;
(e)	Co-operation with producers, processors and the trade generally to ensure as far as possible a balance between production and available markets;
(f)	To encourage the organization of producers of livestock for regular and orderly marketing;
(g)	Improvement of quality of all livestock;
(h)	The formation of a uniform policy on external marketing, with a view not only to promoting new, but also retaining and developing, existing markets;
(i)	The stabilization of supplies, and the regulation of quality to each particular market;
(j)	The utilization of all available means to secure fair returns to the primary producers of good stock.
In the past speeches have been made in this house by various hon. members to draw attention to the fact that, as far as the producer of livestock is concerned, he is at the mercy of the packers.

I note that clause 2 (a) refers to "agricultural product", defining it in this way-
-livestock (including fur-bearing animals raised in captivity), eggs, poultry, milk, vegetables, fruit, honey and maple syrup, and products thereof;
My understanding is that this would mean any product that contained any part of any of these commodities; for instance, any product that contained a portion of meat or that contained any fruit, or that contained any vegetable. Is that what it means? Then it says, "and products thereof". I was thinking of margarine; would it come under this provision? It is partly a livestock product, partly a vegetable product or partly a grain product.

Clause 3 (1) (c) says the governor in council may make regulations to-
-prescribe fees that may be charged for the grading or inspection of agricultural products;
What does this refer to, and who would pay the fees?
